From 764814d7ff25b6c0e7f9c8ad0f03000a88923fd4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ultimatejeeves <ultimatejeeves@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 12:28:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11/43] Web: GS thread yields to its event loop; rebuild lost
 WebGL contexts; per-frame timings; even pacing

- The browser recycles each presented picture's GPU buffer (transferToImageBitmap) on the presenting
  thread's event loop. The GS worker never returned to it, so every picture leaked a frame of video
  memory: 27 GB within 3 minutes at 2x, then the GPU process died and the picture went black while the
  game kept running. The GS loop now runs in slices (until a picture goes out, or 50 ms) and yields
  through a MessageChannel. GPU memory now stays flat (~95 MB) instead of growing ~70 MB/s.
- The GS checks its context every picture; a lost context (GPU reset, out of video memory) is rebuilt
  on a new OffscreenCanvas (GS memory lives in m_pRAM, the GL side is caches) and the page is told
  through Module.wsxGsEvent. Contexts are dropped on the GS thread: emscripten_webgl_destroy_context
  is proxied to the main thread, which doesn't know this worker's contexts, and never returned.
- WsxFrameStats: per-frame rings in shared memory (VM: frame time, limiter sleep, JIT, disc and GS
  waits; GS: busy/idle per picture), read by the page through getFrameStatsAddress().
- The frame limiter paces right before vblank start, so pictures leave at the deadline instead of at
  deadline + that frame's emulation time.
- debugLoseGsContext() test hook.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
